course: world
lesson: 15
title: Weather: pressure, wind, clouds and storms
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Weather is what the air is like at one place and time: hot or cold, wet or dry, calm or windy, cloudy or clear.
explain: The air above us presses on everything; this push is called air pressure. Warm air is lighter and rises, while cool air is heavier and sinks.
explain: Wind is air moving from places where the pressure is high to places where it is low, like air rushing out of an untied balloon.
explain: Clouds form when moist air rises and cools; when the droplets grow heavy they fall as rain, and when the cloud is cold enough the water falls as snow or hail.
explain: In a storm cloud, bits of ice and water crash together and build up electric charge until a giant spark jumps: that is lightning. It heats the air so suddenly that the air bursts outward with the bang we hear as thunder.
explain: For example, you see the flash before you hear the thunder, because light travels much faster than sound.
fact: Weather is the state of the air at one place and time.
fact: Air pressure is the push of the air above us.
fact: Wind blows from high pressure towards low pressure.
fact: High pressure usually brings calm, dry weather; low pressure often brings clouds, wind and rain.
fact: Snow forms when water freezes into ice crystals inside a cold cloud.
fact: Hail is balls of ice that grow in tall storm clouds, carried up and down by strong winds.
fact: Lightning is a giant electric spark; thunder is the sound of the air it heats suddenly bursting outward.
fact: Light travels much faster than sound, so we see lightning before we hear thunder.
fact: Sound travels about one kilometre in three seconds.
fact: A thermometer measures temperature, a barometer measures air pressure, a rain gauge measures rainfall and a wind vane shows the direction of the wind.
exercise: What is wind? | Air moving from high pressure to low pressure | Air pushed along by trees waving | Clouds pushing the air in front of them | The Earth breathing out
exercise: What does a barometer measure? | Air pressure | Temperature | Rainfall | Wind direction
exercise: What weather often comes with low air pressure? | Clouds, wind and rain | Clear and calm skies | Long, very dry sunny days | A complete stillness
exercise: What is lightning? | A giant electric spark in a storm | Fire falling from the clouds | Sunlight trapped inside a cloud | Clouds rubbing until they glow
exercise: What makes the sound of thunder? | Air heated so suddenly by lightning that it bursts outward | Two clouds bumping into each other | Heavy rain hitting the ground | Strong wind blowing hard through the clouds and over the hills
exercise: Why do we see lightning before we hear thunder? | Light travels much faster than sound | The thunder happens later than the flash | Our eyes work faster than our ears | Sound has to go around the clouds
exercise: How does snow form? | Water freezes into ice crystals inside a cold cloud | Rain freezes after it lands on the ground | Clouds break into small white pieces | Frozen dust falls down from space
exercise: What is hail? | Balls of ice that grow in tall storm clouds | Snowflakes that have half melted | Raindrops that freeze on the ground | Ice blown down from high mountains
exercise: What does a wind vane show? | The direction the wind comes from | How hot or cold the air is right now | How much rain has fallen during the day | How high or low the air pressure is
exercise: What does warm air do? | It rises, because it is lighter than the cooler air around it | It sinks, because heat is heavy and always falls to the ground | It stays still in one place | It only ever moves sideways
exercise: What is weather? | What the air is like at one place and time | The usual conditions over many years | The shape of the land | The season of the year
exercise: About how long does sound take to travel one kilometre? | About three seconds | Only about one second | About ten whole seconds | No time at all, it's instant
exercise: What weather does high air pressure usually bring? | Calm, dry weather | Big, loud thunderstorms | Heavy falls of snow | Strong, howling gales
exercise: Which instrument measures how much rain has fallen? | A rain gauge | A thermometer | A barometer | A wind vane
test: You see a flash and count six seconds before the thunder. About how far away was the lightning? | About two kilometres | About six kilometres | About twenty kilometres | Right above your head
test: A weather report says the air pressure is falling fast. What weather is likely? | Clouds, wind and rain | A long sunny spell | A completely still day | Clearer skies than before
test: Why does air rush out of an untied balloon? | It moves from high pressure inside to lower pressure outside | The rubber pulls air in from outside | The air inside is cooler than the air outside, so it escapes | Air always moves upwards
test: Which is true about thunder? | It is the sound made by lightning | It comes from clouds hitting each other | It is the sound of the wind breaking | It is made by heavy rain on the roof
test: On a hot summer afternoon a tall dark cloud grows very quickly. What might it bring? | A thunderstorm with heavy rain or hail | A light and gentle fall of soft snowflakes | Clear, calm air and a bright, cloudless blue sky | Thick fog like on a cold winter morning
test: If light and sound travelled at the same speed, what would you notice in a storm? | You would hear the thunder at the same moment as the flash | You would never hear thunder at all | Lightning would become invisible | You would hear the thunder before the flash
test: Which tool would tell you whether the wind is blowing from the north? | A wind vane | A barometer | A thermometer | A rain gauge
test: Why does it sometimes snow instead of rain? | The air is cold enough for the ice crystals to stay frozen as they fall | The cloud is very old | The wind is very strong | The cloud floats very high above the sea, where the air is always thin and dry
test: Which sentence describes the weather? | It is raining in our town this morning | Our country has cold winters every year | Deserts are dry most of the year | The poles are always cold
test: lightning just went, how far away is it if thunder came 6 secs later | About 2 kilometres | About 6 kilometres | About 18 kilometres | Right above us
test: wots a barometer for | Measuring air pressure | Measuring temperature | Measuring rainfall | Showing which way the wind blows
test: Leo says thunder is two clouds bumping into each other. true? | No, it's air heated by lightning suddenly bursting outward | Yes, clouds crash together and make the boom | Yes, it's ice in the clouds cracking | No, it's the wind hitting the ground
test: a teacher said wind blows from low pressure to high, did she mean it the other way? | Yes, wind blows from high pressure to low pressure | No, wind really blows from low to high | No, wind does not depend on pressure | Yes, wind blows from hot to cold places only
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the movement of wind? | It proceeds from regions of high pressure towards regions of low pressure | It proceeds from regions of low pressure towards regions of high pressure | It proceeds invariably from north to south | It proceeds from the sea towards the land at all times
exam: Identify the principal reason for which lightning is perceived before thunder. | Light travels considerably faster than sound | Thunder is produced some time after lightning | Sound is obstructed by the clouds | The eyes respond faster than the ears
exam: It is given that thunder is heard nine seconds after lightning is seen. Ascertain the approximate distance of the storm. | Three kilometres | Nine kilometres | One kilometre | Twenty-seven kilometres
exam: Identify the instrument employed for the measurement of air pressure. | A barometer | A thermometer | A rain gauge | A wind vane
